1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,057
Film a lamp or a screen with your phone, and sometimes you see this.

2
00:00:05,557 --> 00:00:07,942
Dark stripes, rolling through the picture.

3
00:00:09,442 --> 00:00:12,955
Yet to your own eyes, the light looks perfectly steady.

4
00:00:13,455 --> 00:00:15,351
So which one is telling the truth?

5
00:00:16,851 --> 00:00:17,718
Both of them.

6
00:00:17,718 --> 00:00:20,519
And the reason starts in your wall socket.

7
00:00:22,319 --> 00:00:25,413
The power in your wall is alternating current.

8
00:00:25,913 --> 00:00:30,960
In Vietnam and Europe, it swings back and forth 50 times a second.

9
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:33,008
In the United States, 60.

10
00:00:33,508 --> 00:00:37,104
Each swing has a push one way, and a push the other way.

11
00:00:37,604 --> 00:00:41,027
A light does not care which way the current flows.

12
00:00:41,027 --> 00:00:42,875
It is bright at both peaks.

13
00:00:44,375 --> 00:00:49,978
So it can peak twice per swing: 100 or 120 times a second.

14
00:00:52,078 --> 00:00:56,775
An old filament bulb stays hot between peaks, so it barely dims.

15
00:00:57,275 --> 00:00:59,375
An LED has no such memory.

16
00:00:59,375 --> 00:01:02,443
It follows its power almost instantly.

17
00:01:02,443 --> 00:01:05,673
When the power dips, the light dips too.

18
00:01:06,173 --> 00:01:10,452
A good LED driver smooths the power, and the light stays steady.

19
00:01:10,952 --> 00:01:16,737
A cheap driver smooths it less, so the light can ripple, sometimes almost to dark.

20
00:01:18,237 --> 00:01:22,158
Dimming adds another trick, called pulse-width modulation.

21
00:01:22,658 --> 00:01:28,561
The LED switches fully on and off, very fast, and stays off longer to look dimmer.

22
00:01:29,061 --> 00:01:34,340
Many phone screens, and some laptop screens, dim this way at low brightness.

23
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:38,564
So why do you not see any of this?

24
00:01:39,064 --> 00:01:45,670
Your eyes and brain add up light over a short moment, and blend fast changes into one glow.

25
00:01:46,170 --> 00:01:51,047
For most people, flicker faster than about 80 times a second looks steady.

26
00:01:52,547 --> 00:01:59,462
Some people still notice it, especially on dimmed screens, or when their eyes dart across a light.

27
00:02:01,562 --> 00:02:03,597
A phone camera works differently.

28
00:02:04,097 --> 00:02:07,820
Most phone cameras do not take the whole picture at once.

29
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:12,580
They read the sensor one row at a time, from top to bottom.

30
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:14,963
This is called a rolling shutter.

31
00:02:16,463 --> 00:02:21,625
Each row collects light for a very short moment, especially in bright scenes.

32
00:02:22,125 --> 00:02:24,628
Rows caught at a peak come out bright.

33
00:02:24,628 --> 00:02:26,934
Rows caught in a dip come out dark.

34
00:02:27,434 --> 00:02:31,335
Stack the rows, and the flicker becomes stripes across the picture.

35
00:02:32,835 --> 00:02:37,356
Your eyes never see them, because they never look one row at a time.

36
00:02:39,156 --> 00:02:40,833
But why do the stripes move?

37
00:02:41,333 --> 00:02:47,608
Say your light pulses 100 times a second, and your camera records 30 frames a second.

38
00:02:48,108 --> 00:02:51,274
That is 3⅓ pulses per frame.

39
00:02:51,274 --> 00:02:54,145
That leftover third is the whole story.

40
00:02:54,645 --> 00:03:00,405
So each new frame starts a third of a pulse later in the light's rhythm, and the stripes shift.

41
00:03:01,905 --> 00:03:07,898
Frame after frame, they roll, like the wheels that seem to spin backwards on camera.

42
00:03:08,398 --> 00:03:12,214
Now try 120 pulses at 30 frames.

43
00:03:12,214 --> 00:03:16,966
That is exactly 4 per frame, so the stripes almost stand still.

44
00:03:19,066 --> 00:03:22,417
The fix is to let every row collect whole pulses.

45
00:03:23,917 --> 00:03:25,457
Use a pro camera mode.

46
00:03:25,457 --> 00:03:31,969
Where the power is 50 Hz, set the shutter to 1/50 or 1/100 of a second.

47
00:03:32,469 --> 00:03:37,253
Where it is 60 Hz, use 1/60 or 1/120 of a second.

48
00:03:38,753 --> 00:03:43,489
Now every row gets the same amount of light, and the stripes fade away.

49
00:03:44,989 --> 00:03:50,129
Many phones try to do this on their own, with a setting called anti-banding.

50
00:03:51,929 --> 00:03:54,522
So the stripes are not a fault in your camera.

51
00:03:55,022 --> 00:03:58,916
They are the light's real rhythm, the one your eyes smooth over.

52
00:04:00,416 --> 00:04:04,155
Point your phone at your lights tonight, and see which ones pulse.
